# 11. Token and cost control

The largest mistake would be repeatedly sending the entire dog history and full video to a frontier model.

## Use a structured dog-state summary

```json
{
  "dog_id": "dog_123",
  "age_months": 18,
  "priority_goal": "loose_leash",
  "risk_flags": [],
  "current_protocol": "loose_leash_001",
  "current_level": 3,
  "last_five_session_summary": {
    "success_rate": 0.72,
    "reward_delay_median_ms": 1430,
    "taut_leash_events_per_minute": 3.1
  }
}
```

## Video efficiency

For a 30-second clip:

1. perform local motion and quality analysis;
2. retain keyframes plus short bursts around detected events;
3. send high-resolution crops only when needed;
4. submit one compressed clip or event package to the VLM;
5. request strict JSON output;
6. cache results;
7. do not reprocess unless the rubric changes.

Google documents approximately 300 tokens per video second at default media resolution and roughly 100 tokens per second at low resolution, reinforcing the need for selective analysis. ([Google AI for Developers][1])

