# 13. Privacy and compliance design

Video from inside a home can reveal people, children, voices, addresses, routines and sensitive personal context. The system should therefore treat training media as high-sensitivity personal data even though the primary subject is a dog.

The EU AI Act sits alongside existing data-protection and consumer-protection law; it does not replace GDPR obligations. It also emphasises trustworthy, human-centred AI, transparency, documentation and risk management. ([EUR-Lex][8])

Required controls:

* explicit AI disclosure;
* separate consent for video analysis;
* separate consent for trainer sharing;
* no facial recognition;
* no identity inference;
* blur human faces and unrelated people where feasible;
* audio transcription opt-out;
* short default raw-video retention;
* delete raw video after derived events are created, unless the user saves it;
* no provider training on customer content where contractually avoidable;
* EU or Swiss processing region where possible;
* data-processing agreements;
* subprocessor register;
* export and deletion;
* household-member permissions;
* immutable consent records;
* model and rule audit trail;
* DPIA before broader deployment;
* child/bystander handling policy;
* incident response procedure.

## Recommended retention

* rejected or unusable upload: 24 hours;
* raw free-tier video: seven days;
* paid video: 30 days by default;
* derived observations: retained with training history;
* trainer-shared copy: user-controlled and documented;
* model debugging samples: explicit separate opt-in only.

